;;; Ein stdlib — `std.debts`: what an obligation requires and what pays it, as
;;; facts, and `(debts-discharged)` where nothing required is unpaid.
;;;
;;; An obligation says what a state still **owes**: `std.algebra`'s
;;; `total-owed` owes each member of a relation's first label a partner, for
;;; as long as the state holds none. What it concludes is `(open R)`, a fact
;;; of the state that owes, written at its fixpoint where no fork inherits it,
;;; once a relation (Q-M1f.566): a child that pays the debt holds none. A rule
;;; that reads it makes its program not monotone (Q-M1f.569).
;;;
;;; This module states the two halves of a debt that **can** be facts, because
;;; each stays true as a state grows:
;;;
;;;     (debt-due  R J)     the requirement: the obligation's trigger holds for J
;;;     (debt-paid R J)     the payment: the trigger holds, and a witness is held
;;;
;;; `J` is the instance, a fact no state holds, which names the obligation,
;;; its activator's arguments and what its trigger bound:
;;; `(total-owed-of seats is-a Guest Chair Ann)` is *`seats` owes Ann a
;;; Chair, by `total-owed` over `is-a`*. What a state owes is then a
;;; difference, the requirements with no payment beside them, and a rule can
;;; read both sides. Of the two verdicts over them, the one that stays true
;;; as the state grows is a fact too:
;;;
;;;     (debts-discharged)  no requirement is unpaid
;;;
;;; A requirement is never retracted and a payment never withdrawn, so where a
;;; state holds `(debts-discharged)` every larger state holds it. It is to
;;; *discharged* what `(false)` is to *dead*: the verdict that can be stored.
;;;
;;; ── the four obligations of the library, restated ───────────────────────────
;;; Each obligation has a rule for its requirement and one for its payment,
;;; with the obligation's own parameters:
;;;
;;;   std.algebra  total-owed       total-due,       total-paid       (?R ?isa)
;;;   std.algebra  surjective-owed  surjective-due,  surjective-paid  (?R ?isa)
;;;   std.slots    slot-owed-room   room-due,        room-paid        (?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index)
;;;   std.slots    slot-owed-fill   fill-due,        fill-paid        (?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index)
;;;
;;; None of the eight reads an `absent`: a requirement is the obligation's
;;; trigger, and a payment is the trigger with the witness the obligation's
;;; `absent` looks for.
;;;
;;; ── usage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; One fact asks for all of it:
;;;
;;;     (import std.debts)
;;;     (debts-of-owed)
;;;
;;; Where the program holds `(debts-of-owed)`, `debts-of-total`,
;;; `debts-of-surjective`, `debts-of-room` and `debts-of-fill` each read an
;;; obligation's activator, `(total-owed seats is-a)`, written or derived, and
;;; activate its pair of rules with the same arguments; and
;;; `discharged-from-nothing-unpaid` concludes `(debts-discharged)`. A program
;;; can also activate a pair by hand, `(total-due seats is-a)` and
;;; `(total-paid seats is-a)`, listing the two names; nothing then concludes
;;; `(debts-discharged)`.
;;;
;;; **`(debts-discharged)` is about the requirements that are stated.** It
;;; agrees with what the engine says a state owes where every obligation the
;;; program activates is one of the four. An obligation of the program's own
;;; is restated by two rules of the program's own that conclude `debt-due`
;;; and `debt-paid`; one left unstated is owed by the state and unknown to
;;; `(debts-discharged)`.
;;;
;;; `discharged-from-nothing-unpaid` reads an `absent` inside an `absent`, a
;;; *for every requirement, a payment*, which a larger state can only turn
;;; from failing to holding, so the static check keeps calling the program
;;; monotone. It is judged at the boundary, when the closure is at its
;;; fixpoint, as every `absent` is.
;;;
;;; ── the relations are no module's ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;; `debt-due`, `debt-paid`, `debts-discharged`, the four instance relations
;;; and `debts-of-owed` are relations the module writes or reads and does not
;;; declare, so a program writes each as it is, with nothing to list, as it
;;; writes `(bijective R)` for `std.algebra`. A declaration would bring its
;;; labels to every program that imports the module, as names, and a relation
;;; of two labels would need its held extent closed against the blind
;;; enumerator, a fact a program's rule can read: an import would then move
;;; what a program that asked for nothing holds. Undeclared, none of the
;;; relations is one the blind enumerator guesses about.
;;;
;;; ── what an import brings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; `std.algebra` and `std.slots`, whole (Q-M1f.247): the obligations' names
;;; are theirs. Of its own, thirteen rules and nothing else: no relation and
;;; no fact. Each rule acts under its activator or where the program holds
;;; `(debts-of-owed)`, so a program that imports the module beside those two
;;; and asks for nothing holds what it held.
;;;
;;; ── The names this module occupies ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;; The rules `total-due`, `total-paid`, `surjective-due`, `surjective-paid`,
;;; `room-due`, `room-paid`, `fill-due`, `fill-paid`, `debts-of-total`,
;;; `debts-of-surjective`, `debts-of-room`, `debts-of-fill` and
;;; `discharged-from-nothing-unpaid`. It writes `debt-due`, `debt-paid`,
;;; `debts-discharged`, `total-owed-of`, `surjective-owed-of`,
;;; `slot-owed-room-of` and `slot-owed-fill-of`, and reads `debts-of-owed`,
;;; which are no module's: a program that uses one of those eight words for a
;;; relation of its own shares it with these rules.

(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed surjective-owed))
(import std.slots   :symbols (slot-owed-room slot-owed-fill))

;; ── std.algebra's pair ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; `total-owed` is
;;   (relation ?R ?A ?B) ∧ (?isa ?a ?A) ∧ absent((?isa ?b ?B) ∧ (?R ?a ?b)):
;; the requirement is what stands before the `absent`, and the payment is that
;; with what the `absent` looks for. The instance is written under
;; `total-owed-of`, the obligation's parameters and then what its trigger
;; binds, so that no fact stands under the obligation rule's own name.
(rule total-due (?R ?isa)
  :match  (and (relation ?R ?A ?B)
               (?isa ?a ?A))
  :assert (debt-due ?R (total-owed-of ?R ?isa ?A ?B ?a))
  :why    "{?R} is required to give {?a} a {?B}.")

(rule total-paid (?R ?isa)
  :match  (and (relation ?R ?A ?B)
               (?isa ?a ?A)
               (?isa ?b ?B)
               (?R ?a ?b))
  :assert (debt-paid ?R (total-owed-of ?R ?isa ?A ?B ?a))
  :why    "{?R} gives {?a} the {?B} {?b}.")

(rule surjective-due (?R ?isa)
  :match  (and (relation ?R ?A ?B)
               (?isa ?b ?B))
  :assert (debt-due ?R (surjective-owed-of ?R ?isa ?A ?B ?b))
  :why    "{?R} is required to give {?b} a preimage in {?A}.")

(rule surjective-paid (?R ?isa)
  :match  (and (relation ?R ?A ?B)
               (?isa ?b ?B)
               (?isa ?a ?A)
               (?R ?a ?b))
  :assert (debt-paid ?R (surjective-owed-of ?R ?isa ?A ?B ?b))
  :why    "{?R} gives {?b} the preimage {?a}.")

;; ── std.slots' pair ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; `slot-owed-room` owes each member of a kind under `?super`, the index kind
;; apart, a slot; `slot-owed-fill` owes each slot a member of each such kind.
(rule room-due (?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index)
  :match  (and (?isa ?a ?Ta)
               (?sub ?Ta ?super)
               (neq ?Ta ?index))
  :assert (debt-due ?R (slot-owed-room-of ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index ?a ?Ta))
  :why    "{?a} is required to be in some {?index}.")

(rule room-paid (?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index)
  :match  (and (?isa ?a ?Ta)
               (?sub ?Ta ?super)
               (neq ?Ta ?index)
               (?isa ?i ?index)
               (?R ?a ?i))
  :assert (debt-paid ?R (slot-owed-room-of ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index ?a ?Ta))
  :why    "{?a} is in {?i}.")

(rule fill-due (?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index)
  :match  (and (?isa ?i ?index)
               (?sub ?Tv ?super)
               (neq ?Tv ?index))
  :assert (debt-due ?R (slot-owed-fill-of ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index ?i ?Tv))
  :why    "slot {?i} is required to have a {?Tv}.")

(rule fill-paid (?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index)
  :match  (and (?isa ?i ?index)
               (?sub ?Tv ?super)
               (neq ?Tv ?index)
               (?isa ?v ?Tv)
               (?R ?i ?v))
  :assert (debt-paid ?R (slot-owed-fill-of ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index ?i ?Tv))
  :why    "slot {?i} has the {?Tv} {?v}.")

;; ── every activated obligation at once ───────────────────────────────────────
;; No parameter: each reads the fact `(debts-of-owed)`, which a program writes
;; to ask for all of them, and one obligation's activator.
(rule debts-of-total ()
  :match  (and (debts-of-owed) (total-owed ?R ?isa))
  :assert (and (total-due ?R ?isa) (total-paid ?R ?isa))
  :why    "{?R} owes each member a partner over {?isa}: its requirements and payments are stated.")

(rule debts-of-surjective ()
  :match  (and (debts-of-owed) (surjective-owed ?R ?isa))
  :assert (and (surjective-due ?R ?isa) (surjective-paid ?R ?isa))
  :why    "{?R} owes each member a preimage over {?isa}: its requirements and payments are stated.")

(rule debts-of-room ()
  :match  (and (debts-of-owed) (slot-owed-room ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index))
  :assert (and (room-due ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index) (room-paid ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index))
  :why    "{?R} owes each member a {?index}: its requirements and payments are stated.")

(rule debts-of-fill ()
  :match  (and (debts-of-owed) (slot-owed-fill ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index))
  :assert (and (fill-due ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index) (fill-paid ?R ?isa ?sub ?super ?index))
  :why    "{?R} owes each {?index} a member of each kind: its requirements and payments are stated.")

;; ── discharged ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; For every requirement, a payment: the `absent` of a requirement that has no
;; payment. It holds of a state that requires nothing.
(rule discharged-from-nothing-unpaid ()
  :match  (and (debts-of-owed)
               (absent (and (debt-due ?R ?j) (absent (debt-paid ?R ?j)))))
  :assert (debts-discharged)
  :why    "every requirement is paid.")
